-   "source": "### Optuna study — XGBoost with coupled depth/leaves\n\nSearches XGBoost on `fe_v4_native` — the minimal feature set (the three engineered\ncolumns `AverageMonthly`, `contract_x_payment`, `contract_x_internet` on top of the\nbase categoricals). XGBoost consumes the category-dtype columns directly via\n`enable_categorical=True` (with the `hist` tree method), so there is no encoding step.\n\n**`max_leaves` × `max_depth` coupling.** By default XGBoost grows *depthwise* — it\nsplits every node level by level up to `max_depth`, and `max_leaves` (default 0 =\nunlimited) never binds, so total leaf count is not a tunable knob at all. To make the\ndepth/leaves interaction a real lever — the same one the LightGBM study exploited —\nthis study sets `grow_policy='lossguide'`, which grows *leaf-wise* (expand the\nhighest-gain leaf first, like LightGBM). Now `max_leaves` is the primary capacity\ncontrol and `max_depth` is a guard rail. The two are coupled exactly as in the\nLightGBM study: a depth-*d* tree holds at most 2^*d* leaves, so any\n`max_leaves ≥ 2^max_depth` is non-binding. `max_depth` is suggested first and\n`max_leaves` is drawn from [4, min(2^`max_depth`, 255)], so every sampled value is\nlive and the TPE sampler wastes no trials on dead leaf-count space.\n\nTrial 0 re-enqueues the best XGBoost config to date (run `20260610-051307-62d4fa`,\n`xgb-refined-best-fe_v3`, `max_depth=6`) translated into the lossguide regime with\n`max_leaves = 2^6 = 64` — a full depth-6 tree, so the leaf cap is non-binding and the\nseed reproduces that depthwise-equivalent model. (The feature set differs from that\nrun's fe_v3, so the inner-CV score won't match exactly; the seed just starts the\nsearch from a strong, sensible point rather than cold.)\n\nBudget: 60 trials × 3-fold CV on 594k rows — roughly 20–40 min on CPU.\n\nThe run config below pins `run_config['params']` to `xgb_study.best_params`, so run\nthis study first, then the run cell."
